;;; Syntax probe — arg-kinds.
;;;
;;; Every argument kind a fact can carry: a name, digits (a name, so `7` and
;;; `07` are two), `1..5` and `1..*` (names: Ein has no range token, and
;;; the name rule reads each as the name it spells), a name
;;; with connectors and decorators, a nested fact, and a stored negation as a
;;; value. A string, the bare `_` and `?_` are refused at the parse
;;; (Q-M1e2.22 for the `_`), and the fixture holds none of them.
;;;
;;; Its `(solve …)` holds `r` out of the hypotheses: the fixture's point is
;;; the node kinds as written, and unscoped its universe's commitments would
;;; pass the state route's 2^128 limit. R is {∅}: `ein solve` reports no
;;; candidate, so R is decided by root alone, and root is alive and owes
;;; nothing (the read-out's rows `universe — no candidate` and `∅ in R —
;;; root is alive and owes nothing`).
;;;
;;; One of the eighteen node-kind fixtures. As corpus entries they are
;;; rendered under every parse view and every op, and each rendering is
;;; digested by `corpus_shapes.md5`, so a change to how any of these
;;; shapes is drawn moves a digest. They exist because no *puzzle*
;;; contains these shapes.

(relation r T T :why "{?3} r {?4}")
(r 7 07)
(r 1..5 1..*)
(r $x a-b_c.d)
(r (nested a b) c)
(r (not (nested a b)) c)

(solve :no-hypothesis (r))
